WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.348 --> 00:00:01.669
- Good morning, Believe Nation,

00:00:01.669 --> 00:00:04.790
today's message is experiment.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.480
Over to you, Elie Goulding.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.663
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.663 --> 00:00:12.163
♫ I wake up every morning
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:18.316
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.316 --> 00:00:20.956
- It's key to everything,
or it's key to what I do.

00:00:20.956 --> 00:00:24.794
I wouldn't be here without
experimenting like crazy.

00:00:24.794 --> 00:00:29.279
I like so many different
types of music and I hope that

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:32.409
I could get away with including
all of those different

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.702
influences on my first
record, and I think I got away

00:00:34.702 --> 00:00:37.305
with it and maybe on my second as well.

00:00:37.305 --> 00:00:41.736
But I mean like, I would
say that I'm a pop artist

00:00:41.736 --> 00:00:45.069
and play pop music, but it's not without

00:00:46.354 --> 00:00:50.521
a lot of experimentation and
also risking writing something

00:00:51.858 --> 00:00:55.941
that maybe isn't very good
or is silly, you know.

00:00:56.914 --> 00:01:00.754
My voice is my instrument,
so sometimes I sing things

00:01:00.754 --> 00:01:05.463
that just sound crazy, but
then one take out of 20

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:07.888
will be really, really
cool and I keep that one

00:01:07.888 --> 00:01:09.875
and then that's that,
so yeah, experimentation

00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:11.985
is everything for me as a musician.

00:01:11.985 --> 00:01:14.165
- I think extermination
is that the key to success

00:01:14.165 --> 00:01:16.810
not just as a musician, not
just as an entrepreneur,

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.250
but in general, in life,
you need to experiment.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.394
You need to take the ideas
that you have in your head

00:01:22.394 --> 00:01:24.685
and go out and try stuff because

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:26.500
if you are not experimenting,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:27.550
then what's happening?

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.984
Then you're just living life by the book,

00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:33.831
you're just doing things that
other people tell you to do.

00:01:33.831 --> 00:01:36.938
You're doing the same things
over and over and over

00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:39.593
and over and over again,
you're not trying anything new

00:01:39.593 --> 00:01:42.867
and so your life becomes
this boring repetition day

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.654
in and day out and that sucks,
especially as a creative

00:01:46.654 --> 00:01:49.006
entrepreneur who wants to go
out and make a difference.

00:01:49.006 --> 00:01:51.231
So I want to address what
I feel holds people back

00:01:51.231 --> 00:01:53.294
from trying from experimenting.

00:01:53.294 --> 00:01:55.194
The first part is I think
people are just afraid

00:01:55.194 --> 00:01:56.970
of being judged, they're
afraid of failing,

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.403
they're afraid of putting
themselves out there

00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:01.605
and trying something new
and it not working out

00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:03.049
and people are going to laugh at them

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:05.032
or they're going to be embarrassed

00:02:05.032 --> 00:02:09.004
and that is not the way to live your life.

00:02:09.004 --> 00:02:12.030
If you look at successful
people, the further and further

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:15.958
ahead they get, right, the more
and more success they have,

00:02:15.958 --> 00:02:19.490
you'll find that those
super successful people,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.715
they don't care about how
other people perceive them.

00:02:22.715 --> 00:02:26.712
I am not doing what I do
because I'm worried about

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:28.582
other people's judgements.

00:02:28.582 --> 00:02:29.739
And the people who are at the bottom,

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:31.230
the people who never get ahead,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.452
the people who continually struggle,

00:02:34.452 --> 00:02:37.430
are the ones who are always
afraid of how other people

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.957
are going to judge them.

00:02:38.957 --> 00:02:41.141
You're going to make mistakes.

00:02:41.141 --> 00:02:43.782
You will try things that will not work out

00:02:43.782 --> 00:02:46.875
and the key is, you need to
start building that muscle.

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:50.643
You have to start building
that experimentation muscle,

00:02:50.643 --> 00:02:53.116
because when you put something
out there and it doesn't work

00:02:53.116 --> 00:02:54.563
what happens is you grow stronger.

00:02:54.563 --> 00:02:56.214
When you take an idea from your head

00:02:56.214 --> 00:02:58.482
and you put it out there and you try,

00:02:58.482 --> 00:03:01.090
every time you do that, you grow stronger.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.990
Every time you care a little bit less

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.803
about somebody else's opinion
of you, you grow stronger,

00:03:05.803 --> 00:03:08.564
and you have a choice every single day.

00:03:08.564 --> 00:03:10.677
Every single day you have a choice

00:03:10.677 --> 00:03:13.452
to either grow stronger
or shrink back down.

00:03:13.452 --> 00:03:18.093
Every time you get an idea
and you do something about it,

00:03:18.093 --> 00:03:20.478
even if it doesn't
work, you grow stronger,

00:03:20.478 --> 00:03:23.239
you build that muscle
so you can do it again

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:25.345
and next time with more power.

00:03:25.345 --> 00:03:27.772
Every time you get an idea
and you don't do something

00:03:27.772 --> 00:03:31.003
about it and you shrink
down and you tell yourself

00:03:31.003 --> 00:03:33.195
why you can't do it,
and you're worried about

00:03:33.195 --> 00:03:35.556
other people's judgements,
then you get weaker

00:03:35.556 --> 00:03:37.861
and you reinforce that story.

00:03:37.861 --> 00:03:41.499
Either way, you're reinforcing
a story to yourself

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:43.925
that you can do this
and you can be strong,

00:03:43.925 --> 00:03:47.231
or you can't do this and you're weak.

00:03:47.231 --> 00:03:49.920
And every day you have an
opportunity to reinforce

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.461
either story, and I want
you to start playing here.

00:03:54.461 --> 00:03:56.763
I want you to start feeling
like you are powerful.

00:03:56.763 --> 00:03:58.713
I want you to stop caring
about what other people think

00:03:58.713 --> 00:04:01.529
of you and I want you to
start, when you get an idea,

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:03.148
doing something about it.

00:04:03.148 --> 00:04:04.962
And it doesn't just happen
overnight, at least from

00:04:04.962 --> 00:04:07.043
my experience it isn't
just like you know what?

00:04:07.043 --> 00:04:09.477
Tomorrow, I don't care
what anybody thinks of me.

00:04:09.477 --> 00:04:11.910
You could say that, but
actually believe it?

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:12.903
I don't know.

00:04:12.903 --> 00:04:14.974
But you can do it in steps.

00:04:14.974 --> 00:04:18.856
You may not be able to go
and lift this huge weight

00:04:18.856 --> 00:04:21.210
but you can work your way
up there and every day

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.394
you get a little bit stronger
and so every little tiny

00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:29.055
micro-win is still a win
that makes you stronger

00:04:29.055 --> 00:04:31.127
and lets you move one step forward.

00:04:31.127 --> 00:04:33.063
The second thing about
experimentation I think

00:04:33.063 --> 00:04:35.292
holds people back is they
feel it has to be perfect,

00:04:35.292 --> 00:04:38.558
it has to be the perfect thing,
it has to be all worked out

00:04:38.558 --> 00:04:42.161
before they ever get started,
and that is not the path.

00:04:42.161 --> 00:04:44.720
What you have to understand
is most of the learning

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.342
that you'll get, most of the
figuring out what it's going to be

00:04:48.342 --> 00:04:50.880
comes from actually trying it.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.656
Whatever idea you have, you
can't just come up with it

00:04:53.656 --> 00:04:55.388
in your head and write it
down on a piece of paper,

00:04:55.388 --> 00:04:58.318
you have to go and start
doing it and you'll learn

00:04:58.318 --> 00:05:01.207
by the doing of how to adjust.

00:05:01.207 --> 00:05:02.981
So as an example, one
of my instructors here

00:05:02.981 --> 00:05:05.540
trying to dance salsa
wanted us to practice

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:08.388
storytelling in our classes,
so instead of coming in

00:05:08.388 --> 00:05:11.071
and just teaching how
to do a turn in salsa,

00:05:11.071 --> 00:05:13.720
she's giving a story around
it, maybe it's her own story

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.808
of how she learned, maybe
it's another student's story

00:05:16.808 --> 00:05:18.749
about how they learned it,

00:05:18.749 --> 00:05:20.074
and the difficulties they may have had,

00:05:20.074 --> 00:05:23.160
and how they came through
it, because her goal

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.551
in being an instructor here
is not just to teach people

00:05:25.551 --> 00:05:28.025
how to be dancers and
learn a certain move,

00:05:28.025 --> 00:05:31.331
but to build people's confidence,
to strengthen their soul,

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:34.058
to become better humans, to
take what they learned here,

00:05:34.058 --> 00:05:36.733
the feeling they get, and
have that have a ripple effect

00:05:36.733 --> 00:05:38.837
out on the rest of their
life, that's her goal,

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:40.444
that's what she wants for her students.

00:05:40.444 --> 00:05:44.423
She wants to be one of the
best instructors of all time

00:05:44.423 --> 00:05:47.729
for her students because
she had a profound impact

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:52.359
on their life, and not just
teaching them subject matter.

00:05:52.359 --> 00:05:54.306
And so she experimented, she had this idea

00:05:54.306 --> 00:05:56.939
and she did something
about it and at the start,

00:05:56.939 --> 00:05:58.543
it wasn't working so well.

00:05:58.543 --> 00:06:01.202
Her stories were long, they
didn't have the right theme,

00:06:01.202 --> 00:06:03.596
it was hard for people to
understand, she was summarizing

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.401
instead of going deep into the material.

00:06:06.401 --> 00:06:09.987
It wasn't a great first start,
but that's to be expected.

00:06:09.987 --> 00:06:12.365
And she got some feedback and
there was one particularly

00:06:12.365 --> 00:06:14.765
harsh message that came from
one of the students that said,

00:06:14.765 --> 00:06:17.168
"I would rather go with
this other instructor

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:21.335
because this one is, you spend
too much time storytelling,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:26.395
and I'm not getting value from
it," and I showed it to her

00:06:26.395 --> 00:06:29.411
and it was really, really
you know, hard to take in

00:06:29.411 --> 00:06:31.675
because when you know that
you're not doing a good job

00:06:31.675 --> 00:06:33.742
and then somebody shows it to you, right,

00:06:33.742 --> 00:06:36.891
here's what this person
said, it can be really hard,

00:06:36.891 --> 00:06:39.860
but to her credit she didn't
stop and she kept working

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.143
and she kept getting better

00:06:41.143 --> 00:06:42.743
and she kept practicing
and she kept adjusting

00:06:42.743 --> 00:06:45.385
and she continued to
experiment, to the point where,

00:06:45.385 --> 00:06:48.793
earlier this week, she
had an amazing class.

00:06:48.793 --> 00:06:52.617
One where everybody left on such a high

00:06:52.617 --> 00:06:53.692
that they're going to remember

00:06:53.692 --> 00:06:55.588
for the rest of their week,
the rest of their month,

00:06:55.588 --> 00:06:59.405
maybe the rest of the year,
that class they had with her

00:06:59.405 --> 00:07:03.222
and she transformed in the
class from just being a dance

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:06.069
teacher to somebody who was
having a profound impact

00:07:06.069 --> 00:07:08.125
on the lives of her students

00:07:08.125 --> 00:07:10.550
and that happens in
everything that you do.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:14.099
You are going to suck
at the start, expect it.

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:16.442
That's okay, but don't
let that hold you back

00:07:16.442 --> 00:07:18.842
from trying and experimenting.

00:07:18.842 --> 00:07:21.110
You go out out there, you
try something, you adjust,

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.179
you tweak it to see if you like it.

00:07:23.179 --> 00:07:25.528
So many of you have
these plans in your head

00:07:25.528 --> 00:07:26.936
of these great ideas that you have

00:07:26.936 --> 00:07:28.548
and you do nothing with them.

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.298
Get them out and try.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.264
Stop worrying about what people think.

00:07:33.264 --> 00:07:36.477
Build that muscle that
gets stronger, and realize

00:07:36.477 --> 00:07:38.832
that the learning comes from the doing,

00:07:38.832 --> 00:07:40.478
not just the thinking.

00:07:40.478 --> 00:07:42.582
So the question then today is I'm curious

00:07:42.582 --> 00:07:45.642
what are you guys
experimenting with right now

00:07:45.642 --> 00:07:47.864
that's pushing you to be better,

00:07:47.864 --> 00:07:50.506
that's pushing through some
limited beliefs you might have.

00:07:50.506 --> 00:07:52.947
What's the hardest thing you're
experimenting on right now.

00:07:52.947 --> 00:07:55.018
Leave it in the comments below,

00:07:55.018 --> 00:07:56.429
I'm going to join in on the discussion.

00:07:56.429 --> 00:07:58.651
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Norz Szanto.

00:07:58.651 --> 00:08:00.919
Nora, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:03.106
Your One Word, I really, really, really,

00:08:03.106 --> 00:08:05.505
really appreciate it and
I hope you're enjoying.

00:08:05.505 --> 00:08:07.485
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:08:07.485 --> 00:08:09.498
I believe in you and I hope you continue

00:08:09.498 --> 00:08:12.934
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is

00:08:12.934 --> 00:08:14.212
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:08:14.212 --> 00:08:16.773
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:08:16.773 --> 00:08:19.471
♫ I wake up every morning

00:08:19.471 --> 00:08:23.016
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:08:23.016 --> 00:08:24.915
Hey Believe Nation, if
you want a little bit more

00:08:24.915 --> 00:08:26.557
inspiration on experimenting,

00:08:26.557 --> 00:08:29.948
I think these bonus clips will help you.

00:08:29.948 --> 00:08:32.185
- I stopped listening
to music after Kurt died

00:08:32.185 --> 00:08:34.008
because it was so
emotional just hearing it.

00:08:34.008 --> 00:08:35.903
I didn't want to hear it
on the radio, I didn't want

00:08:35.903 --> 00:08:38.403
to play it, I didn't want
to join another band,

00:08:38.403 --> 00:08:42.969
and then after a while I realize
that it was the one thing

00:08:42.969 --> 00:08:45.785
that was going to help
me get through everything

00:08:45.785 --> 00:08:48.137
so I started writing lyrics
and I started writing songs

00:08:48.137 --> 00:08:49.541
and I went and recorded this demo tape.

00:08:49.541 --> 00:08:51.534
♫ When I talk about it

00:08:51.534 --> 00:08:55.451
♫ Carries on reasons only knew

00:08:56.612 --> 00:08:58.098
♫ When I talk about it

00:08:58.098 --> 00:08:59.711
A record company called and said,

00:08:59.711 --> 00:09:02.060
"Hey, we want to release your record."

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.084
I said, "That's a demo, that's not a band.

00:09:04.084 --> 00:09:07.642
That's a thing I did
by myself in five days.

00:09:07.642 --> 00:09:08.968
- And you play every instrument?

00:09:08.968 --> 00:09:11.467
- Yeah, so then I started to think,

00:09:11.467 --> 00:09:12.828
Wow, maybe I will try this.

00:09:12.828 --> 00:09:15.178
I've never been the singer of a band.

00:09:15.178 --> 00:09:17.654
- Was it a tough transition
from drummer to front man?

00:09:17.654 --> 00:09:21.821
- Absolutely, it took years,
man, it took a long time.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:24.843
- It's interesting the way it starts,

00:09:24.843 --> 00:09:26.002
there's some moaning at the beginning.

00:09:26.002 --> 00:09:27.162
- Of which one?

00:09:27.162 --> 00:09:28.401
- The first song on the record.

00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:29.397
- Birthday.

00:09:29.397 --> 00:09:30.671
- Don't know the titles,
no one told me the titles,

00:09:30.671 --> 00:09:31.703
just heard the record, it is called,

00:09:31.703 --> 00:09:33.149
look at that exclusive, perfect.

00:09:33.149 --> 00:09:34.686
(laughter)

00:09:34.686 --> 00:09:36.380
Yeah, you said birthday in it a lot.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.529
But that's a very mature
progression sonically

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:41.954
to add those moaning sounds as
opposed to being that Disney

00:09:41.954 --> 00:09:44.487
person you've been known
to be over the years.

00:09:44.487 --> 00:09:46.264
- Sure, but I mean I'm

00:09:46.264 --> 00:09:47.930
- [Interviewer] It's not a bad thing.

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:51.902
- No, of course, I think
it's more me being able to

00:09:51.902 --> 00:09:54.097
experiment, you know, going into the booth

00:09:54.097 --> 00:09:56.369
and actually having the
confidence of actually being like,

00:09:56.369 --> 00:09:58.842
"Alright, let me try this,
let me see if this works,"

00:09:58.842 --> 00:10:02.239
and messing around with
melodies or just harmonies,

00:10:02.239 --> 00:10:04.492
or as you say, moaning.

00:10:04.492 --> 00:10:05.934
- It was, isn't it, kind of ?

00:10:05.934 --> 00:10:08.203
- I guess that's the right
term for it in a way.

00:10:08.203 --> 00:10:09.234
- [Interviewer] I think so.

00:10:09.234 --> 00:10:11.259
- But yeah, no in a way,
I think I really was

00:10:11.259 --> 00:10:13.848
able to just play and that was fun.

00:10:13.848 --> 00:10:16.371
- You write in your book,
'cause you're talking

00:10:16.371 --> 00:10:19.430
about the art of writing and
planning routes and everything,

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.415
you say, "Magic exists, but
often requires some planning."

00:10:22.415 --> 00:10:24.853
But you were talking about
writing, I think it seems also

00:10:24.853 --> 00:10:27.807
apply to the music even
like you have some planning,

00:10:27.807 --> 00:10:29.119
but there's that room for improv.

00:10:29.119 --> 00:10:30.312
- Well said, absolutely so.

00:10:30.312 --> 00:10:32.345
Yeah, all that effort of
study and I always said

00:10:32.345 --> 00:10:36.378
that the early records that
the three of us made together,

00:10:36.378 --> 00:10:38.492
we were experimenting, we
were learning how to play

00:10:38.492 --> 00:10:40.259
our instruments, we were
learning how to write songs,

00:10:40.259 --> 00:10:42.988
we were learning how to
arrange, especially through say,

00:10:42.988 --> 00:10:45.300
Farewell to Kings Hemispheres,
we were learning so much

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.536
about arrangement and
orchestration in the sense

00:10:47.536 --> 00:10:52.137
of a three piece rock band
all of that gathered together

00:10:52.137 --> 00:10:56.431
and then by say, Moving Pictures was 1980,

00:10:56.431 --> 00:10:58.589
so we've been together
six years by that time

00:10:58.589 --> 00:11:02.097
and then we gelled and
all of those studies,

00:11:02.097 --> 00:11:04.077
all of those courses of
study that we went through

00:11:04.077 --> 00:11:07.729
together with pure innocence
and pure enthusiasm

00:11:07.729 --> 00:11:11.116
suddenly they became a unified
approach that Moving Pictures

00:11:11.116 --> 00:11:14.954
and from then on carried for
us and then as that time,

00:11:14.954 --> 00:11:17.971
even from that time, yeah,
we continued to experiment

00:11:17.971 --> 00:11:19.459
absolutely with different kinds of songs,

00:11:19.459 --> 00:11:20.737
different kinds of arrangements,

00:11:20.737 --> 00:11:23.257
orchestrations of all
kinds, different producers,

00:11:23.257 --> 00:11:24.575
there's another good example.

00:11:24.575 --> 00:11:26.999
You can get comfortable
working with the same producer

00:11:26.999 --> 00:11:28.861
and some people do their whole career

00:11:28.861 --> 00:11:31.629
but we always felt we
wanted new brains to pick

00:11:31.629 --> 00:11:34.766
and new criticisms and new enthusiasms,

00:11:34.766 --> 00:11:36.266
just new opinions.

00:11:38.903 --> 00:11:43.897
- In late 2012, I worked on a
book launch for Tim Ferriss.

00:11:43.897 --> 00:11:46.257
He wrote a book called The Four Hour Chef.

00:11:46.257 --> 00:11:49.396
It was his third book, it
was published by Amazon

00:11:49.396 --> 00:11:51.456
and because it was published
by Amazon, we found out

00:11:51.456 --> 00:11:54.437
at the very last minute,
about six weeks before launch,

00:11:54.437 --> 00:11:57.454
that it was going to be banned
by every major bookstore

00:11:57.454 --> 00:12:00.642
in the United States
and this is pretty much

00:12:00.642 --> 00:12:02.550
the worst thing that can happen to you

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:04.207
if you're doing a traditional book launch,

00:12:04.207 --> 00:12:05.700
'cause that's where books are sold.

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:08.549
So how do we hit The New
York Times Best Seller List?

00:12:08.549 --> 00:12:11.773
How do we move copies if all
the traditional marketing stuff

00:12:11.773 --> 00:12:13.292
is out the window?

00:12:13.292 --> 00:12:16.195
That leaves growth hacking, right?

00:12:16.195 --> 00:12:18.768
How do we do this, how do we
treat this book like a start up

00:12:18.768 --> 00:12:20.665
that we're creating from nothing?

00:12:20.665 --> 00:12:23.420
One of the first things we did
was a bit torrent giveaway.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.509
We gave away something
like 250 pages of the book.

00:12:26.509 --> 00:12:30.100
Videos, photos, all sorts of extra stuff.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.850
It ended up getting downloaded I think

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:34.355
about three million times.

00:12:34.355 --> 00:12:37.752
It drove 250,000 people
to the Amazon page.

00:12:37.752 --> 00:12:39.860
It was the single most effective thing.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.088
I've worked on close
to a dozen bestsellers.

00:12:42.088 --> 00:12:45.305
It was the single most effective
thing that I've ever seen

00:12:45.305 --> 00:12:48.787
in the history of book marketing,
and it came from giving

00:12:48.787 --> 00:12:53.381
a sizeable portion of the book
away and so it was this proof

00:12:53.381 --> 00:12:56.027
for me that the traditional marketing,

00:12:56.027 --> 00:13:00.390
the untrackable, the untested
things aren't working anymore

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.089
and we want to experiment,
and we want to try new things.

